/-!
# Lemma I.4.17 for arbitrary `q ≥ 2`: the iterated first-hit fresh diagram family

Source: T. Hutchcroft, *Critical long-range percolation I: High effective dimension*,
arXiv:2508.18807v1, Lemma I.4.17 and its proof, supplied paper text lines 2444--2513
(`repo/.scratch/formalization-next/paper.txt`):

  "We obtain a bound in terms of tree diagrams with two pinned vertices at `x` and `y`, with
  `2p + 2q + 1` edges in total, some number `3 ≤ ℓ ≤ p + q + 1` of them belong to the path
  connecting `x` and `y`."

The marked (base) side carries `2p + 1` edges, so the *fresh* side carries exactly `2q` edges.
The fresh event is production's `I417Gen.freshMark y g j w`
(`Correlation/I417/General/Defs.lean`): the two halves `y → w` and `w → g j` of a simple open path
occur as a `DisjointOccurrence`, and all `q` labelled arms `g 0, …, g (q-1)` lie in the fresh
cluster of `y`.

## What this file defines

The fresh diagrams are rooted binary trees with the pin `y` at the root and the `q` *labelled*
arms as leaves (`2q - 1` edges), with the mark `w` subdividing one edge of the path from `y` to
the selected arm: `2q` edges in all.  They are generated by iterating the inside-segment
attachment of the accepted `q = 4` artifact (`smwx8o5tv/m2/Q4`, `splitAt`/`step`): starting from
the two legs `y → w`, `w → g j`, each of the remaining `q - 1` arms is attached at a first-hit
vertex inside the current diagram, and each attachment replaces one leg by two and appends the
new arm leg, so the index type grows `ι → ι ⊕ Bool` and the leg count grows `2 ↦ 4 ↦ … ↦ 2q`.

* `Idx n` is the index type after `n` attachments: `Idx 0 = Fin 2` (the two halves of the
  selected path) and `Idx (n+1) = Idx n ⊕ Bool`.  It has `2 * (n + 1)` elements.
* `Hist V n` records an attachment history of length `n`: a recursive list whose `t`-th entry is
  the split leg `k : Idx t`, the first-hit vertex `z`, and the attached arm `d`.  This is the
  leg-order/vector model: the `n`-th record is appended to the history, and `Hist.arm` reads the
  arms back in the chosen order `Fin n → V`.
* `Hist.legs H y w ga : Idx n → V × V` is the resulting `2 * (n + 1)`-leg diagram.
* `genShape y w g j e` is the union of `ShapeOfI (Hist.legs H y w (g j))` over all histories `H`
  of length `q - 1` whose `t`-th attached arm is `g (e t)`, where `e : Fin (q-1) ≃ {i // i ≠ j}`
  enumerates the `q - 1` arms other than the selected one.  The union therefore keeps *all* arm
  labels and *all* `q - 1` arm orderings explicitly.

All degeneracies (`w = y`, `w = g j`, coincident arms, attachment points at existing vertices,
trivial legs) are absorbed by the vertex sums and by the fact that a trivial walk is a legal
witness.  Every definition of this development lives in this file; `Attach`, `Cover` and `Bound`
contain only theorems.

The construction is deliberately index-generic (`splitAt`/`Diag` are polymorphic in the index
type), so it covers the `q = 2` base and the `q → q + 1` step uniformly; `q = 4` is the instance
already verified in the accepted artifact, and `q = 5` is obtained by one more `Hist.snoc`.
-/

open MeasureTheory Measure ProbabilityTheory unitInterval
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

set_option linter.unusedSectionVars false
set_option linter.style.longLine false
set_option linter.style.haveILetI false
set_option linter.unusedDecidableInType false

@[expose] public section

namespace PercLib.CriticalLRP.I417GenIter

export PercLib.CriticalLRP.I417FreshFour.Refined
  (LegWalk ShapeOfI shapeOfI_fin splitAt splitAt_inl_self splitAt_inl_of_ne splitAt_inr_false
   splitAt_inr_true baseLegs baseWalks baseWalks_zero baseWalks_one Diag baseDiag)

open PercLib.CriticalLRP

variable {V : Type*} [Countable V] [DecidableEq V]

universe u

attribute [local instance] Classical.propDecidable

/-! ## The growing index type, the attachment history, and the general family -/

/-- **The index type after `n` attachment rounds.**  `Idx 0 = Fin 2` (the two halves of the
selected path) and each attachment grows the type by `⊕ Bool`, adding the two halves of the split
leg and the new arm.  Hence `Fintype.card (Idx n) = 2 * (n + 1)`; see `card_Idx`. -/
def Idx : ℕ → Type
  | 0 => Fin 2
  | n + 1 => Idx n ⊕ Bool

/-- `Idx n` is finite, with `2 * (n + 1)` elements. -/
instance instFintypeIdx (n : ℕ) : Fintype (Idx n) := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact inferInstanceAs (Fintype (Fin 2))
  | succ n ih =>
    letI := ih
    exact inferInstanceAs (Fintype (Idx n ⊕ Bool))

/-- The index that carries the pin `y`: the leftmost slot of the diagram.  Splitting reuses the
slot of the split leg for the half that keeps its source, so this slot always starts at `y`. -/
def first : (n : ℕ) → Idx n
  | 0 => (0 : Fin 2)
  | n + 1 => Sum.inl (first n)

/-- **An attachment history of length `n`.**  Each `Hist.snoc H k z d` records one first-hit
attachment: `k` is the leg of the current diagram that contains the hit point `z`, and `d` is the
attached arm.  This is the explicit leg-order model: the history appends one record per round, so
the arms are attached in the order recorded by `Hist.arm : Fin n → V`. -/
inductive Hist (V : Type u) : ℕ → Type u
  | nil : Hist V 0
  | snoc : {n : ℕ} → Hist V n → Idx n → V → V → Hist V (n + 1)

/-- Attachment histories are countable when `V` is: a successor history is a finite tuple of a
previous history, a finite split index, and two vertices, so it injects into a countable product. -/
instance instCountableHist (n : ℕ) : Countable (Hist V n) := by
  induction n with
  | zero =>
    exact Function.Injective.countable
      (f := fun _ : Hist V 0 => ()) (fun a b _ => by cases a; cases b; rfl)
  | succ n ih =>
    haveI := ih
    refine Function.Injective.countable
      (f := fun H : Hist V (n + 1) => match H with | .snoc H k z d => (H, k, z, d)) ?_
    intro a b h
    cases a with
    | snoc Ha ka za da =>
      cases b with
      | snoc Hb kb zb db =>
        simp only [Prod.mk.injEq] at h
        obtain ⟨rfl, rfl, rfl, rfl⟩ := h
        rfl

/-- The legs of the diagram built by an attachment history. -/
noncomputable def Hist.legs : {n : ℕ} → Hist V n → V → V → V → Idx n → V × V
  | _, .nil, y, w, ga => baseLegs y w ga
  | _, .snoc H k z d, y, w, ga => splitAt (H.legs y w ga) k z d

/-- The arms attached by a history, in order: `Hist.arm H t` is the `d` of the `t`-th
attachment. -/
def Hist.arm : {n : ℕ} → Hist V n → Fin n → V
  | _, .nil, t => Fin.elim0 t
  | _, .snoc H _k _z d, t => Fin.lastCases d (fun t' => H.arm t') t

/-- **The general fresh diagram family at `q`.**  The union of `ShapeOfI (Hist.legs H y w (g j))`
over all attachment histories `H` of length `q - 1` whose `t`-th attached arm is the `t`-th label
of the enumeration `e : Fin (q-1) ≃ {i : Fin q // i ≠ j}` of the arms other than `j`.

Every member has `2q` legs and keeps the selected arm `g j` on the marked path `y → w → g j`;
the other `q - 1` arms are attached in the order `e`, and every ordering is available by varying
`e`. -/
def genShape (y w : V) {q : ℕ} (g : Fin q → V) (j : Fin q)
    (e : Fin (q - 1) ≃ {i : Fin q // i ≠ j}) : Set (Config V) :=
  ⋃ H : {H : Hist V (q - 1) // ∀ t : Fin (q - 1), H.arm t = g (e t).1},
    ShapeOfI (H.1.legs y w (g j))

/-- **The remaining diagram-evaluation step at general `q`**, as a single named `Prop` with no
analytic wrapper.  `GenFreshEval π q C` says that for every constant row sum `χ` of the two-point
kernel, every `y, w`, every arm tuple `g`, every selected arm `j` and every ordering `e` of the
other arms, the general fresh family is bounded by the printed normalisation

  `∑_{ℓ∈[1,q]} C ℓ · χ^(2q-ℓ) · T^ℓ(w,y)`.

`C ℓ` is the number of diagrams of the family whose mark `w` has `y`-depth `ℓ`; the finite count
`card_histChoices`/`card_histChoices_four` supplies the total `∑_ℓ C ℓ = 2^(q-1) (q-1)!` of branch
choices, and `card_genLegs` supplies the exponent split `(2q - ℓ) + ℓ = 2q`.  The computational
companion `Gen/verify_gen_family.py` computes `C` for `q ≤ 5` (reproducing the accepted `q = 4`
profile `(6,12,15,15)` exactly); the substitution `∑_v τ(a,v) = χ` at the attachment vertices is
the diagram-evaluation step still to be proved.  This is the general-`q` analogue of the accepted
artifact's `FreshFourEval`. -/
noncomputable def GenFreshEval (π : Sym2 V → unitInterval) (q : ℕ) (C : ℕ → ℕ) : Prop :=
  ∀ (χ : ℝ≥0∞), (∀ v : V, ∑' z : V, (edgeMeasure π) (connSet v z) = χ) →
    ∀ (y w : V) (g : Fin q → V) (j : Fin q)
      (e : Fin (q - 1) ≃ {i : Fin q // i ≠ j}),
      (edgeMeasure π) (genShape y w g j e) ≤
        ∑ ℓ ∈ Finset.Icc 1 q,
          (C ℓ : ℝ≥0∞) * (χ ^ (2 * q - ℓ) *
            I417Base.iterPow (fun a b => (edgeMeasure π) (connSet a b)) ℓ w y)

end PercLib.CriticalLRP.I417GenIter
